==============================================================================
0) INTUITION — WHY THIS TOPIC EVEN EXISTS
==============================================================================

WHY THIS CONCEPT EXISTS
Real systems grow.

At start:
- 1 DB node
- 1 cache server
- 1 queue consumer

Then traffic explodes:
- WhatsApp → billions of chats
- Instagram → billions of posts
- Uber → millions of ride events
- Netflix → massive watch history
- Amazon → global cart + inventory traffic

One machine cannot hold:
- all data
- all QPS
- all memory
- all CPU

So we must SPLIT data.

This splitting is called:
PARTITIONING / SHARDING.

But now the real problem starts:

> If we add/remove machines, how do we move data without moving EVERYTHING?

This exact pain leads to:
CONSISTENT HASHING.

REAL LIFE ANALOGY
Think of lockers in a gym.

Naive system:
- locker = user_id % N

If lockers increase from 10 → 11,
almost everyone gets a new locker.

Total chaos.

Consistent hashing says:
- arrange lockers in a ring
- each user picks nearest locker clockwise
- adding 1 locker only affects nearby users

This is why it scales beautifully.

Golden intuition:
> Consistent hashing minimizes reshuffling during topology changes.

==============================================================================
1) WHY NORMAL HASHING FAILS
==============================================================================

WHY THIS EXISTS
First idea most engineers think:

hash(key) % number_of_servers

Example:
- 4 cache nodes
- key = "user:123"
- hash % 4 = 2

Works fine...
until cluster changes.

PROBLEM
If nodes become 5:
hash % 5 changes for almost every key.

Result:
- almost full cache miss
- massive DB thundering herd
- huge data movement
- rebalance storm

Real world impact:
Imagine Redis cluster for Instagram feed cache:
adding 1 node invalidates almost all hot cache keys.

Latency spike.
DB melts.

Tradeoff:
simple but terrible elasticity.

WHEN NOT TO USE
Never use modulo hashing when:
- cluster size changes often
- auto-scaling enabled
- ephemeral nodes
- distributed cache
- distributed storage

==============================================================================
2) CORE IDEA — CONSISTENT HASHING
==============================================================================

WHY THIS EXISTS
We want:
- easy scaling
- minimal key movement
- stable partition ownership

CORE IDEA
Both:
- nodes
- keys

are hashed into the SAME HASH SPACE (ring).

Example hash space:
0 → 2^32 - 1

Imagine circle:
0 ... max ... wraps around

Each key belongs to:
> first node clockwise on the ring

WHY CLOCKWISE RULE
Simple deterministic ownership.

Solves:
- routing
- lookups
- rebalance
- failover ownership

Architecture decision:
ring-based ownership.

Tradeoff:
possible skew without virtual nodes.

==============================================================================
3) ALGORITHM — HOW IT WORKS STEP BY STEP
==============================================================================

PHASE 1: PLACE NODES
Hash node IDs:
- nodeA → 100
- nodeB → 400
- nodeC → 700

PHASE 2: PLACE KEY
hash("user:123") = 430

Move clockwise:
430 → next node = nodeC(700)

So key belongs to nodeC.

WHY THIS DESIGN
Only local interval changes matter.

Mathematical beauty:
ownership interval = previous_node_exclusive → current_node_inclusive

This makes rebalancing extremely cheap.

TIME COMPLEXITY
Lookup:
- O(log N) using sorted ring
- O(1) average with optimized routing table

Production implementations:
- binary search over sorted token list
- skip lists
- B-tree metadata
- routing map cache

==============================================================================
4) ADDING A NEW NODE — REBALANCING
==============================================================================

WHY THIS CASE MATTERS
Real systems auto-scale constantly.

Example:
Netflix cache cluster adds more memcached nodes during prime time.

Suppose new nodeD hashes to 500.

OLD:
B(400) → C(700)

NEW:
B(400) → D(500) → C(700)

WHAT MOVES
Only keys in range:
(400, 500]

move from C → D

THIS IS THE MAGIC
Only neighboring interval moves.

Not entire cluster.

WHY THIS IS HUGE
Benefits:
- less network traffic
- lower rebalance latency
- fewer cache misses
- lower recovery blast radius

Tradeoff:
still needs streaming migration + ownership metadata updates

Real world:
Cassandra token rebalance
DynamoDB partition split concepts
Redis cluster slot migration

==============================================================================
5) REMOVING A NODE
==============================================================================

WHY THIS MATTERS
Machines crash.
ASG terminates nodes.
Spot instances disappear.

Suppose nodeD(500) removed.

Its interval:
(400, 500]

moves to next clockwise node = C

WHY THIS IS GOOD
Again:
only local data moves.

This is fault tolerant scaling.

Failure recovery strategy:
- replicate to successors
- promote replica
- update ring metadata
- replay missed writes

This is where replication deeply combines with consistent hashing.

