==============================================================================
7) VIRTUAL NODES (VERY IMPORTANT SENIOR TOPIC)
==============================================================================

WHY THIS EXISTS
Without vnodes:
real node positions may cluster unevenly.

Example:
one node owns 40%
another owns 5%

Hotspot disaster.

SOLUTION
Each physical node owns multiple virtual points.

Example:
NodeA:
- token1 = 100
- token2 = 350
- token3 = 800

WHY THIS SOLVES PROBLEM
Load becomes statistically balanced.

Advantages:
- smoother rebalance
- better hotspot spread
- faster replacement
- heterogenous node capacity support

Tradeoff:
more metadata
slightly higher routing complexity

Real systems:
- Cassandra
- Dynamo
- Riak

Golden interview line:
> Virtual nodes convert node-level imbalance into token-level smoothing.

==============================================================================
8) FIXED NUMBER OF PARTITIONS
==============================================================================

WHY THIS DESIGN EXISTS
Instead of node-based ownership,
many systems define:
TOTAL FIXED PARTITIONS.

Example:
Kafka style thinking
or Redis cluster slots
or DynamoDB partitions

Say:
1024 partitions fixed forever.

Keys map to partition:
hash(key) % 1024

Then partitions map to machines.

WHY THIS IS BRILLIANT
Node add/remove changes:
partition → machine mapping only

NOT key → partition mapping

Huge operational win.

Example:
Partition 300 moves nodeA → nodeD

Only that partition migrates.

Real analogy:
Apartment numbers fixed,
residents (servers) managing them can change.

==============================================================================
9) IF TOO FEW FIXED PARTITIONS
==============================================================================

WHY THIS IS BAD
Example:
only 4 partitions.

Problems:
- coarse balancing
- hotspot impossible to smooth
- limited parallelism
- large partition migration size

Example:
Instagram celebrity post all likes in partition 2
→ single node overloaded

Tradeoff:
simple metadata
bad fine-grained scaling

WHEN NOT TO USE
Avoid too few when:
- skewed keys
- burst traffic
- uneven workloads
- large clusters

==============================================================================
10) IF TOO MANY FIXED PARTITIONS
==============================================================================

WHY THIS ALSO FAILS
Suppose:
10 million partitions.

Problems:
- metadata huge
- routing memory large
- frequent tiny files
- compaction overhead
- scheduling explosion
- rebalance orchestration complex

Example:
Kafka too many partitions:
- controller pressure
- leader election storms
- open file descriptor issues

Tradeoff:
fine-grained balancing
vs
control plane overhead

Golden line:
> Partition count is a control-plane scalability problem, not just data-plane.

==============================================================================
11) DYNAMIC PARTITIONING
==============================================================================

WHY THIS EXISTS
Workloads are not static.

Today:
10M users

Tomorrow:
500M users

Some keys become hot unexpectedly.

So partition count itself must evolve.

CORE IDEA
Start with reasonable partitions.
Split hot partitions dynamically.

Example:
partition [A-M]
becomes too hot
split into:
[A-G]
[H-M]

WHY THIS IS NEEDED
Solves:
- hotspot
- oversized shards
- uneven storage growth
- write amplification bottlenecks

Real systems:
- Bigtable tablets
- HBase region split
- DynamoDB adaptive partitioning
- Mongo chunk split

==============================================================================
12) HOW DYNAMIC SPLIT WORKS
==============================================================================

STEP 1: detect threshold
Split triggers:
- size > 10GB
- QPS > 50k
- p99 latency breach
- write queue backlog
- compaction pressure

STEP 2: freeze metadata version
Prevent split-brain ownership.

STEP 3: create child partitions
Parent:
[0,100]
Children:
[0,50], [51,100]

STEP 4: migrate data
background copy + dual write

STEP 5: switch routing
atomic metadata flip

WHY DUAL WRITES
Avoid stale writes during migration.

Tradeoff:
temporary write amplification

